00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:30.180
- Culturally

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:34.070
where we, not just in North Carolina,

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:37.200
but again, the culture
that we were a part of

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:38.653
in North Carolina, it was,

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:42.360
you know, "Spare the rod
spoil the child" right.

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:47.200
And so basically everyone
was, again, we're talking

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.633
spanking on the bottom.

00:10:52.072 --> 00:10:53.372
We're limiting it to that.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.120
But then there was this

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.513
sort of--
- An emotion, like not--

00:10:58.513 --> 00:11:00.970
- Not out anger.
- Not out of anger.

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:01.803
- Right.

00:11:01.803 --> 00:11:04.353
- Basically I honestly, the
way that I was brought up,

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:07.350
my brother and I were both spanked.

00:11:07.350 --> 00:11:08.260
It was...

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:12.500
Again, my wife is the one
who's done the research

00:11:12.500 --> 00:11:14.700
and the reading on all this stuff.

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:16.070
Our wives are the one--
- Right.

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:17.920
- That do all of the
reading about parenting

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:21.010
and most of the parenting,
let's just be honest.

00:11:21.010 --> 00:11:21.843
- Yeah.

00:11:21.843 --> 00:11:25.130
- But, and Jesse has
strong feelings about this.

00:11:25.130 --> 00:11:28.850
Basically saying that she feels

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:31.450
she's completely sort of
flipped her perspective

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:34.760
on the role of spanking.

00:11:34.760 --> 00:11:36.450
Now I...

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.220
But just to say that I think that

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:41.950
there's definitely a spectrum

00:11:41.950 --> 00:11:43.690
of how it can be administered, right?

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:45.660
We're not here to defend
it and we're not here.

00:11:45.660 --> 00:11:47.230
Why don't you--
- I'm sorry

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:48.580
I got you into this.
- You brought it up again

00:11:48.580 --> 00:11:51.070
and it's such a controversial thing

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:52.760
that I just don't even want to,

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:53.820
- But it's good illustration
- Talk about it.

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:56.550
- of like, if you got your
first child and she gets spanked

00:11:56.550 --> 00:11:58.380
and then your third
child never gets spanked.

00:11:58.380 --> 00:12:01.867
I mean, it is a good example
of like how things change

00:12:01.867 --> 00:12:03.810
and you learn and

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:04.730
- Well, and we were--
- I'm sorry,

00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:07.080
but if you're the first
child, in one way or another

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:10.510
in every way, you inform what
happens with every other child

00:12:11.719 --> 00:12:13.160
moving forward.

00:12:13.160 --> 00:12:14.080
- I mean,

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.300
I guess what I can say is--
- That seems like it sucks,

00:12:16.300 --> 00:12:17.248
to me.

00:12:17.248 --> 00:12:19.330
- If the same thing was
true in our household

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:22.910
and Locke was the one who was, you know.

00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:27.310
We were undergoing, sort of,
our own personal evolution.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:30.330
Our worldview was being
turned completely upside down

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:33.170
as our kids grew up, which
is a whole different,

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:34.821
a whole different thing.

00:12:34.821 --> 00:12:35.690
- That's true.
- And studies will be done

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:37.560
about kids in this generation
(Rhett chuckles)

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:39.040
because there are so many people

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:41.610
whose worldviews are changing right now.

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:44.280
But setting that aside,
if you just isolate that,

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:48.010
the way that we punished
Locke, both the frequency,

00:12:48.010 --> 00:12:49.770
the intensity

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:52.420
was definitely, we were
more lenient with Shepard,

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:54.880
but it wasn't just because
he was a younger child,

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:56.550
it was because we were changing the way

00:12:56.550 --> 00:12:58.220
that we were thinking about parenting.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:12:59.880
And I can say that thus far--

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:01.220
- And discipline specifically.

00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:04.290
- There doesn't seem to
be a notable difference

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:07.240
between the two of them
in that particular.

00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.557
It's more determined by their disposition

00:13:09.557 --> 00:13:10.880
and their personality

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:13.410
and less the way that we
are interacting with them.

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:16.570
That's another thing that
I think has shifted for me

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:17.590
over time.

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:20.153
And I do think that, the
science shows this is that,

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:25.070
one of the ideas from the
culture that we came from

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:26.850
is that the parents have...

00:13:27.750 --> 00:13:31.670
The weight of responsibility,
like your kids,

00:13:31.670 --> 00:13:34.560
I mean, literally, just
Bible verses that talk about

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:38.540
if you train a child up in
the way that they should go,

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:41.250
they will not depart from it
when they become an adult.

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:43.880
And so, there's actually a lot of shame

00:13:45.192 --> 00:13:48.380
in a lot of religious circles,

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:53.340
when your kid departs from
that, or your kid ends up being

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:54.703
a hellion when they grow up,

00:13:54.703 --> 00:13:56.923
that means that you did something wrong.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:00.350
I'm kinda coming to the conclusion that,

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:01.890
yes, parenting is very important,

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:06.010
but a lot of it is based on
what you model, not what you say

00:14:06.010 --> 00:14:07.940
and also a lot based on their disposition.

00:14:07.940 --> 00:14:10.670
So, that's a little bit of an aside.

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:14.740
- And we were very focused
on, even at the beginning,

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:16.180
based on the books that we were reading.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:17.307
I remember there was a book called,

00:14:17.307 --> 00:14:19.380
"Shepherding a Child's Heart,"

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:21.200
which I thought was very helpful.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:23.080
I don't remember the specifics of it now,

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:24.397
so I'm not recommending it now.

00:14:24.397 --> 00:14:26.163
It was so long ago,

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:30.850
you know, 17, 16 years ago.

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:33.970
But being focused on
not just getting a kid

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:38.970
to robotically respond and do
what we want the kid to do,

00:14:39.880 --> 00:14:44.880
but understand our children's
hearts and who they are

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:50.890
and how to shepherd their
heart into blossoming

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:55.340
as a loving individual

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:56.330
and, you,
- Yeah.

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:57.690
- a well-developed...

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:02.360
And probably, yeah, at certain
times, it boils down to just

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:04.363
well-mannered
(Link chuckles)

00:15:04.363 --> 00:15:05.420
kid.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.140
So--
- But this wasn't.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:07.973
I gotta say--

00:15:07.973 --> 00:15:09.950
- I can't think of another trial,

00:15:09.950 --> 00:15:11.740
some other things that I changed.

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:14.713
- This wasn't true for
me as a child though.

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:17.440
It's almost like...

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:20.880
I mean, my dad was so consistent.

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:22.930
He was so consistent.

00:15:22.930 --> 00:15:23.763
And my mom.

00:15:23.763 --> 00:15:24.596
Both of them

00:15:24.596 --> 00:15:26.090
were so consistent--
- Hm mm.

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:28.790
- In what they thought
and how they parented

00:15:28.790 --> 00:15:31.500
from the beginning until we left.

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.500
And I don't,

00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:35.620
I never perceived a noticeable change

00:15:35.620 --> 00:15:37.010
between the way that I was treated

00:15:37.010 --> 00:15:39.310
and the way that my brother was treated.

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:40.900
Now--
- Hmm?

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:44.130
So you don't think Cole
would say he was the trial

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:45.550
and then they adjusted it for you?

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:48.200
- That may have been happening,
but I never sensed it.

00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:49.880
- Right.
- Looking back at it.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:51.500
I don't have any tangible
examples of that,

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:56.190
but for my kids, it's more of a 100% yes.

00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:59.880
The leniency that we operate with towards

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:05.250
Shepard is definitely higher
than what we did with Locke

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:07.890
and it's based less on, I mean,
I think it's a combination

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:10.430
of just like, you don't
have so much attention

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:11.570
now there's two of them.

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:13.050
I can only devote so
much attention to them.

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:14.183
But also it's just,

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:17.340
this didn't seem to work that well.

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:18.440
He didn't listen to all.

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:19.273
He didn't listen.
(Rhett chuckles)

00:16:19.273 --> 00:16:21.540
He didn't do all this stuff
that we asked him to do.

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:24.650
So, why don't we just let
the second one just be

00:16:24.650 --> 00:16:26.083
a little bit more free.

00:16:27.321 --> 00:16:32.277
- @Takingcareofmomma tweeted
back at us as an oldest child,

00:16:33.177 --> 00:16:35.877
"That stereotype of the first
born being more cautious

00:16:35.877 --> 00:16:38.837
"and law abiding, totally
true in my experience.

00:16:38.837 --> 00:16:41.797
"I would even go so far as to
say that I did a terrible job

00:16:41.797 --> 00:16:44.267
"of breaking in my parents
before my sister got old enough

00:16:44.267 --> 00:16:46.650
"to break the rules."

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:49.660
Yeah, like I said, we had lots of rules

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:52.370
and parameters

00:16:53.570 --> 00:16:54.403
for Lily.

00:16:54.403 --> 00:16:57.580
And I think that, you
know, grow up in that world

00:16:57.580 --> 00:16:59.270
and you're like, okay, this is...

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:01.810
You know, she responded to it.

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:06.810
So, she became the reliable
rule-follower that then,

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:14.430
kind of, as Lincoln and
Lando entered the scene,

00:17:14.430 --> 00:17:15.370
she was...

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:18.950
Sometimes we'd treat
her like a third adult.

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:23.470
A lot of first, oldest children
responded and said that

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:28.470
they're the third parent,
the babysitter all the time.

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:32.430
Being given substantial responsibility

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:35.810
to take care of the siblings
and to follow those rules

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:38.960
and to be reliable and counted on

00:17:38.960 --> 00:17:40.590
is something that I--
- Well, and

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:42.350
the negative aspect of that--
- It just practically,

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:43.183
it plays out that way.

00:17:43.183 --> 00:17:46.050
- But the negative aspect of
that is the bossiness, right?

00:17:46.050 --> 00:17:46.883
Like I say,
- Yeah.

00:17:46.883 --> 00:17:49.230
- I seem to remember Lily going through

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:51.030
the bossy, older sister phase
- Hm mm.

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:54.520
- which definitely seems
like she's well out of that--

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:55.353
at this point.
- Yeah.

00:17:55.353 --> 00:17:59.757
Every everything that we said
to her, then she was like,

00:17:59.757 --> 00:18:01.887
"Oh, here's a younger kid in the house,"

00:18:01.887 --> 00:18:04.997
"now I can say it to
Lincoln," you know, "I can..."

00:18:05.907 --> 00:18:07.220
And can you fault her?

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:09.570
It's like, she's reinforcing the things

00:18:09.570 --> 00:18:10.610
that we would have said

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:12.630
to Lincoln too--
- Well, we've had to say.

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:15.660
I can't tell you how many
times we've had to say...

00:18:15.660 --> 00:18:17.520
This is not quite as common as it was,

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:21.070
maybe even a year ago,
but for several years

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:26.070
Jesse would have to say, "Locke,
I'm parenting right now."

00:18:26.450 --> 00:18:27.470
You know,
(Link chuckles)

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:31.347
because Shepard would be getting
in trouble for something,

00:18:31.347 --> 00:18:34.310
she would be saying something,
I would be saying something

00:18:34.310 --> 00:18:37.150
and then Locke was like,
"Yeah and," and he's like,

00:18:37.150 --> 00:18:38.180
he gets in on it,

00:18:38.180 --> 00:18:39.013
right?
(Link chuckles)

00:18:39.013 --> 00:18:40.430
So, no, no, no, that's not your role.

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:42.070
Your role is not to parent.

00:18:42.070 --> 00:18:43.493
But specifically, we're talking about

00:18:43.493 --> 00:18:45.940
with this whole
rule-follower thing, again,

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:48.000
I think this is where it comes down to

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:52.650
your specific personality
and we're going to,

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:53.670
we keep promising it.

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:55.670
We're going to do the
Enneagram episode, right,

00:18:55.670 --> 00:18:57.270
But--
- Yes.

00:18:57.270 --> 00:19:01.486
- So kids are different (Rhett
chuckles) and recognizing,

00:19:01.486 --> 00:19:03.840
like the fact that Locke,
as I've said before,

00:19:03.840 --> 00:19:06.150
is more of a challenger.

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:10.100
So, when he sees a rule,
he questions the rule.

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:12.530
It's not just the rule
exists, you follow it.

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:15.407
The rule exists, his
immediate response is,

00:19:15.407 --> 00:19:17.837
"Why does that rule exist?"

00:19:17.837 --> 00:19:19.850
"Is that rule actually good?"

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:22.260
Does that rule intend what it is,

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:24.833
what it says that it's
intending to prevent, right?

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:28.810
Whereas Shepard is more just like,

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:30.740
I wouldn't say he's a rule
follower, but I wouldn't say

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:31.990
that he's a rule breaker either.

00:19:31.990 --> 00:19:34.970
I would say that he's
kinda just his own person

00:19:34.970 --> 00:19:36.930
and if the rules suit
him, he'll follow them

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:38.990
and if they don't, he won't.
(Rhett chuckles)

00:19:38.990 --> 00:19:43.990
But again, this doesn't seem
to be true with my kids.

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:47.040
I wouldn't say this one's a
rule follower, this one's not.

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:49.490
And--
- Right.

00:19:49.490 --> 00:19:52.637
- Maybe it's a little bit, I
mean, how would you perceive me

00:19:52.637 --> 00:19:55.293
and my brother growing up?

00:19:56.690 --> 00:19:59.060
You might perceive this a little bit?

00:19:59.060 --> 00:19:59.893
- I mean,

00:20:01.017 --> 00:20:05.300
the consistency of discipline
that you're talking about

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:06.380
does resonate.

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:11.380
I mean, yeah, we would get into trouble

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:13.010
and you would get a punishment

00:20:13.010 --> 00:20:15.790
and then they would call
my mom and then tell her

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:18.960
and then if I was involved
to the same degree,

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:20.640
I would just get the same punishment.

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:22.130
But if they didn't have the conversation,

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:24.290
I wouldn't get any punishment at all.

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:26.070
So I knew that you were getting this.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:27.310
There were always

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:31.410
repercussions for you.

00:20:31.410 --> 00:20:33.060
So.

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:36.900
And yeah, so we would
break the rules together,

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:39.470
but I would, you know, we'd
have to read some momentum

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:40.303
and you would have to

00:20:40.303 --> 00:20:41.970
overcome a lot--
- But versus me

00:20:41.970 --> 00:20:43.943
and my brother--
- But you weren't rebellious.

00:20:43.943 --> 00:20:45.083
If you're like,

00:20:45.083 --> 00:20:46.710
"Oh Cole is,"
- I think you're the same.

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:48.933
I mean, I think his personality is

00:20:48.933 --> 00:20:51.260
probably more of a rule follower,

00:20:51.260 --> 00:20:53.693
but I think because of the
discipline in your home,

00:20:53.693 --> 00:20:58.040
your actions were similar.

00:20:58.040 --> 00:21:02.120
- Yeah, I think we're kinda
the same in this regard.

00:21:02.120 --> 00:21:05.670
I think that compared to--
- It's more about them,

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:06.503
the parents--

00:21:06.503 --> 00:21:07.336
than it is about--
- Yeah, I would say

00:21:07.336 --> 00:21:08.169
compared with--
- your personalities.

00:21:08.169 --> 00:21:11.423
- the general population as kids,

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:14.790
both of us were,

00:21:14.790 --> 00:21:16.140
what you could call a rule-follower.

00:21:16.140 --> 00:21:18.850
Now, we may have been mischievous, right.

00:21:18.850 --> 00:21:20.700
- Yeah.
- Those do some prank calls,

00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:21.533
or whatever.

00:21:21.533 --> 00:21:24.100
Let's listen to, in my
brother's case, it's like,

00:21:24.100 --> 00:21:24.933
I'm gonna get

00:21:25.860 --> 00:21:29.040
the most explicit gangster
rap cassettes available

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:33.030
and then let my brother and
his best friend listen to 'em.

00:21:33.030 --> 00:21:33.920
- Yeah.

00:21:33.920 --> 00:21:36.560
But still, am I going to do the things

00:21:36.560 --> 00:21:40.027
that the adults in my life
expect, at least externally?

00:21:40.027 --> 00:21:40.860
Yes.

00:21:40.860 --> 00:21:42.990
Am I gonna be known as a rebel kid?

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:43.840
No.

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:46.040
And that was the same for both of us.

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:48.710
So this trope didn't really hold true.

00:21:48.710 --> 00:21:51.240
- Let's move on to the middle
child before we forget.

00:21:51.240 --> 00:21:52.950
- Okay.
- I was tempted

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:56.160
to skip middle children entirely

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:58.240
and then at the very end realize it,

00:21:58.240 --> 00:22:01.170
as a joke and then just end the episode,

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:03.270
but let's not do that.

00:22:03.270 --> 00:22:04.510
Let's talk about middle children.

00:22:04.510 --> 00:22:06.950
But first let's sell that T-shirt.

