00:33:17.040 --> 00:33:18.971
Link's gonna have an opinion about

00:33:18.971 --> 00:33:21.130
where this couch is and et cetera

00:33:21.130 --> 00:33:23.500
and I don't have an opinion about it

00:33:23.500 --> 00:33:26.230
and so why didn't he just
make the decision about this.

00:33:26.230 --> 00:33:28.973
- Yeah, @hannahbatch
said, "I'm an only child

00:33:28.973 --> 00:33:33.147
"and my fiance is a middle
child of four and it's perfect.

00:33:33.147 --> 00:33:34.867
"I'm used to getting my way

00:33:34.867 --> 00:33:37.961
"and he's used to letting other
people do what they want."

00:33:37.961 --> 00:33:41.810
So in our dynamic, it does work.

00:33:41.810 --> 00:33:45.010
I mean, if you were a firstborn,

00:33:45.010 --> 00:33:46.319
that would be an interesting test.

00:33:46.319 --> 00:33:47.690
- If we were both

00:33:47.690 --> 00:33:48.523
only children--
- Oh, gosh.

00:33:48.523 --> 00:33:49.356
If we were both

00:33:49.356 --> 00:33:50.189
only children--
- we wouldn't be sitting

00:33:50.189 --> 00:33:51.830
at this table--
- I don't think that.

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:54.400
- I don't think there'll be.

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:55.233
There will be no test at all.

00:33:55.233 --> 00:33:56.970
- And I tend to be really.

00:33:56.970 --> 00:33:59.150
I tend to be a very deferential person

00:33:59.150 --> 00:34:03.530
when it comes to a whole slew of like,

00:34:03.530 --> 00:34:05.080
you know, what things look like,

00:34:05.080 --> 00:34:09.780
what things feel like,
living situation, bathroom.

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:12.349
I tend to be very deferential
about all that stuff.

00:34:12.349 --> 00:34:14.590
I'm....

00:34:14.590 --> 00:34:16.750
But there are things that
I care a whole lot about

00:34:16.750 --> 00:34:20.010
but it tends to be a pretty
narrow spectrum of things

00:34:20.010 --> 00:34:21.510
that I care a whole lot about

00:34:21.510 --> 00:34:25.023
and I kinda put all my
energy into those things.

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:27.330
You know what I'm saying?

00:34:27.330 --> 00:34:29.780
I don't even know exactly
what those things are,

00:34:29.780 --> 00:34:31.650
but I think that's one of the reasons

00:34:31.650 --> 00:34:35.460
that this has not been a lasting
source of conflict for us.

00:34:35.460 --> 00:34:36.293
- Yeah.

00:34:36.293 --> 00:34:39.230
We're in a fortunate birth
order situation here.

00:34:39.230 --> 00:34:41.130
Let's go to youngest children.

00:34:41.130 --> 00:34:42.353
This is you.

00:34:42.353 --> 00:34:44.527
So, we're onto you.

00:34:44.527 --> 00:34:48.257
@RachelBlack said, "I'm the
youngest out of my sister and I

00:34:48.257 --> 00:34:50.737
"and I get away with a lot more,

00:34:50.737 --> 00:34:53.900
"but also don't have the
high expectations put on me

00:34:55.667 --> 00:34:57.317
"which can be both good and bad."

00:34:58.780 --> 00:35:01.470
- I mean, we kind of talked
about this a little bit.

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:02.860
For me this was not the case

00:35:02.860 --> 00:35:05.210
because my parents were super consistent.

00:35:05.210 --> 00:35:08.820
It is kind of the case with our kids,

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:12.260
but I think it's a combination of factors,

00:35:12.260 --> 00:35:13.860
but without a doubt.

00:35:13.860 --> 00:35:16.890
Like what Shepard is allowed to watch,

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:19.167
what Shepherd is allowed to say--

00:35:20.030 --> 00:35:22.163
- Yeah.
- What he's allowed to do,

00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:27.080
is significantly more lenient than...

00:35:27.080 --> 00:35:30.280
I mean, you know, again,

00:35:30.280 --> 00:35:33.233
we come from a conservative
evangelical background.

00:35:34.370 --> 00:35:36.677
Early on, when Locke was really little,

00:35:36.677 --> 00:35:39.033
and he wanted to play with Pokemon cards,

00:35:40.270 --> 00:35:41.810
Jesse had questions about that

00:35:41.810 --> 00:35:45.985
because of the evolution
aspect of Pokemon cards.

00:35:45.985 --> 00:35:50.170
I mean that's how ingrained that idea was.

00:35:50.170 --> 00:35:52.717
Is like, "I don't know about that

00:35:52.717 --> 00:35:54.920
"because I know that those things evolve."

00:35:54.920 --> 00:35:55.800
- Right.
(Rhett laughs)

00:35:55.800 --> 00:35:58.840
Just to give you a little
slice of where we come from.

00:35:58.840 --> 00:36:01.300
- That one's more extreme
than the Harry Potter example,

00:36:01.300 --> 00:36:02.210
which was also true.

00:36:02.210 --> 00:36:04.080
It's like, Harry Potter really took off

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:06.830
before we were having
kids, but we were too old

00:36:06.830 --> 00:36:08.680
to be into it ourselves.

00:36:08.680 --> 00:36:12.470
But eventually Lily got
into it, but it wasn't.

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:16.360
I mean, Lando is reading Harry Potter now

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:19.280
and has been for a year
or so, but when Lily was,

00:36:19.280 --> 00:36:21.950
you know, nine, 10, 11 years old,

00:36:21.950 --> 00:36:23.900
there was no Harry Potter in our house.

00:36:23.900 --> 00:36:24.733
- Right.

00:36:24.733 --> 00:36:26.950
Because of the witchcraft stuff?

00:36:26.950 --> 00:36:30.250
- And because I wasn't into
it and it was a residual

00:36:30.250 --> 00:36:33.010
of that witchcraft stuff
that was just like,

00:36:33.010 --> 00:36:33.843
practically speaking,

00:36:33.843 --> 00:36:35.105
we're not gonna get you--
- But now--

00:36:35.105 --> 00:36:36.080
- into that stuff--
- if I went

00:36:36.080 --> 00:36:37.667
into Shepard's room and he was reading

00:36:37.667 --> 00:36:39.510
"The Adventures of Satan and his Demons,"

00:36:39.510 --> 00:36:41.302
I'd be like, "Are you into that?"

00:36:41.302 --> 00:36:43.520
(Rhett guffaws)
- Right.

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:45.734
- I'm just saying I've completely,

00:36:45.734 --> 00:36:48.990
again, I'm not scared
of any of that stuff.

00:36:48.990 --> 00:36:50.993
I don't believe that it's a reality, so.

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:53.090
In other words, like--

00:36:53.090 --> 00:36:55.210
- And we're not gonna push
our kids towards something

00:36:55.210 --> 00:36:58.080
by trying to, by setting
up the goals for them

00:36:58.080 --> 00:36:58.913
to kick against.

00:36:58.913 --> 00:37:00.150
- Yeah.
- I think that's another part

00:37:00.150 --> 00:37:03.360
of our philosophy that has shifted.

00:37:03.360 --> 00:37:08.360
Is, you know, don't let
fear-drive set up walls.

00:37:11.010 --> 00:37:12.313
- Yeah.
- You know, it's more

00:37:12.313 --> 00:37:14.480
like let's have open
conversations about things

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:18.890
and if there are legitimate
dangers associated with things,

00:37:18.890 --> 00:37:22.310
then let's really get into
the details and discuss it.

00:37:22.310 --> 00:37:26.340
So, it's not about just a blind rule,

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:28.040
or all of a sudden there's a wall here

00:37:28.040 --> 00:37:30.040
and it's like, no questions asked.

00:37:30.040 --> 00:37:32.920
- Well, yeah and to clarify,
I'm using the example

00:37:32.920 --> 00:37:35.000
of "The Adventures of
Satan and his Demons"

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:38.830
as a purposely inflammatory
thing that I know

00:37:38.830 --> 00:37:39.870
is going to upset some people,

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:41.290
because, you know, I like to do that,

00:37:41.290 --> 00:37:44.660
but it's kind of like
- He does not have that book?

00:37:44.660 --> 00:37:45.870
- He does not have that book.

00:37:45.870 --> 00:37:48.410
It was just a funny example.

00:37:48.410 --> 00:37:49.740
But even if you think about,

00:37:49.740 --> 00:37:52.557
let's just talk about the
Lil Nas X-thing for a second

00:37:52.557 --> 00:37:55.880
and the Satanic panic that's
happening around that.

00:37:55.880 --> 00:37:59.110
I do think that, you know, rewind 10 years

00:37:59.110 --> 00:38:00.780
and if my kid.

00:38:00.780 --> 00:38:03.440
And neither one of my kids
would consider themselves

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:06.170
much of a Lil Nas X fan,
but they're both aware

00:38:06.170 --> 00:38:10.293
of the Montero video and the
controversy that it's causing.

00:38:12.568 --> 00:38:14.770
There was a time where
I would have been afraid

00:38:14.770 --> 00:38:16.700
to just let them watch that

00:38:16.700 --> 00:38:19.090
and for us to have a
conversation about it.

00:38:19.090 --> 00:38:23.420
Now, I understand that the
whole point of the video

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:26.840
is to cause the panic
and cause that reaction

00:38:26.840 --> 00:38:28.150
for branding purposes.

00:38:28.150 --> 00:38:30.550
That's what Lil Nas X was
doing from the very beginning

00:38:30.550 --> 00:38:32.960
and a bunch of people took the bait

00:38:32.960 --> 00:38:34.270
and got very upset about it.

00:38:34.270 --> 00:38:35.730
I mean, it was hook, line and sinker,

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:39.110
they just took the bait and
got upset about the video

00:38:39.110 --> 00:38:41.400
and the shoes that had
blood in them, right.

00:38:41.400 --> 00:38:42.233
A lot of people.

00:38:43.810 --> 00:38:45.370
And I think that now it's just like,

00:38:45.370 --> 00:38:49.330
hey, can't we step back and just.

00:38:49.330 --> 00:38:51.347
It isn't like, "Oh,
kids you can do and say

00:38:51.347 --> 00:38:52.530
"whatever you want to."

00:38:52.530 --> 00:38:53.870
It's not that there are no rules.

00:38:53.870 --> 00:38:56.590
It's that the things that are important

00:38:56.590 --> 00:38:59.770
and the things that are
actually going to be

00:39:01.570 --> 00:39:03.730
potentially harmful and dangerous to them,

00:39:03.730 --> 00:39:06.520
let's have real meaningful
conversations about them.

00:39:06.520 --> 00:39:08.310
And let's kind of say
that, hey all these things

00:39:08.310 --> 00:39:10.160
that we thought might be
dangerous and harmful,

00:39:10.160 --> 00:39:12.870
actually aren't and we can
still have a conversation

00:39:12.870 --> 00:39:15.300
about why some people see it that way, so.

00:39:15.300 --> 00:39:20.180
- So with our youngest kids,
there's a lot more leniency

00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:22.770
in our parenting because,

00:39:22.770 --> 00:39:24.540
because we're
(Link chuckles)

00:39:24.540 --> 00:39:25.750
evolving.

00:39:25.750 --> 00:39:29.100
Our perspectives are evolving

00:39:29.100 --> 00:39:34.100
and there's so much that you
wanna protect your kids from

00:39:34.840 --> 00:39:36.280
in every single day.

00:39:36.280 --> 00:39:38.580
There's so much more out there
that they can be exposed to,

00:39:38.580 --> 00:39:39.843
that could be damaging.

00:39:40.918 --> 00:39:44.060
So you've gotta be even more strategic

00:39:44.060 --> 00:39:46.120
in choosing the right battles,

00:39:46.120 --> 00:39:48.393
because if you battle everything,

00:39:49.290 --> 00:39:51.410
there's a sense that it just won't work.

00:39:51.410 --> 00:39:53.700
- And I think that there--
- Nothing will stick.

00:39:53.700 --> 00:39:56.828
- As a very, very broad generalization,

00:39:56.828 --> 00:39:58.950
there are two types of kids, right?

00:39:58.950 --> 00:40:01.680
There are the kids who are going to do

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:04.850
the subversive transgressive things

00:40:04.850 --> 00:40:07.240
and there are kids who
are not going to do them.

00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:09.540
And whether and if you are the kind of kid

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:12.010
who is sort of wired to do
the transgressive things

00:40:12.010 --> 00:40:15.060
and you grow up in a really
conservative atmosphere,

00:40:15.060 --> 00:40:18.350
you are going to break bad
and you're going to break bad

00:40:18.350 --> 00:40:20.573
in a real big way a lot of times.

00:40:21.547 --> 00:40:25.120
But if you're a rule-follower
when you grow up in that,

00:40:25.120 --> 00:40:27.720
you might just get out okay
and just be a rule-follower

00:40:27.720 --> 00:40:29.070
for the rest of your life.

00:40:29.070 --> 00:40:31.000
- Or you might get fixated on it.

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:36.000
Like, I mean, with Lando,
he's at a stage right now

00:40:36.130 --> 00:40:41.130
and we're figuring it out in what it means

00:40:41.220 --> 00:40:43.430
and kinda tracing it back

00:40:43.430 --> 00:40:45.060
and figuring out what our path forward is.

00:40:45.060 --> 00:40:48.990
But he gets worked up about
anything that feels like

00:40:48.990 --> 00:40:51.130
it's a violation of a rule.

00:40:51.130 --> 00:40:55.270
Not like a societal rule,
whether it's a curse word,

00:40:55.270 --> 00:40:59.490
or even anything that's
offensive, or embarrassing,

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:00.910
like a fart--

00:41:00.910 --> 00:41:01.743
- And again,

00:41:01.743 --> 00:41:02.760
that's not something
- You know, he's

00:41:02.760 --> 00:41:04.730
- that came from you?
- No, he's very sensitive.

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:08.850
So for him, it's like, we're
trying to help him understand

00:41:09.780 --> 00:41:14.310
a grid through which to interpret
what is actually dangerous

00:41:16.290 --> 00:41:18.920
what does it mean for
something to be offensive,

00:41:18.920 --> 00:41:21.433
you know, or not.

00:41:22.270 --> 00:41:27.270
So that there's not
unnecessarily tall walls built up

00:41:27.370 --> 00:41:32.370
around him that keeps him
from truly fully experiencing

00:41:32.770 --> 00:41:34.530
his life.
(Link chuckles)

00:41:34.530 --> 00:41:35.664
- Right.

00:41:35.664 --> 00:41:36.497
So, another way

00:41:36.497 --> 00:41:37.330
to say it is--
- So, it's like

00:41:37.330 --> 00:41:38.850
tearing down walls for him.
- We're both,

00:41:38.850 --> 00:41:41.920
I would say, to similar degrees,

00:41:41.920 --> 00:41:46.690
lenient on our youngest
kids, but they operate

00:41:46.690 --> 00:41:50.090
with different levels of personal leniency

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:52.343
because they are different kids.

00:41:52.343 --> 00:41:53.300
- Oh yeah.

00:41:53.300 --> 00:41:56.480
- Again, I just think it
so much has to do with

00:41:56.480 --> 00:41:58.180
just the disposition of the kid.

00:41:58.180 --> 00:41:59.410
It's...

00:41:59.410 --> 00:42:01.270
I don't know, the more I parent

00:42:01.270 --> 00:42:03.279
the more powerless I feel as a parent.

00:42:03.279 --> 00:42:04.112
(both laugh)

00:42:04.112 --> 00:42:05.520
I'm just being honest with you.

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:08.250
That's my experience.

00:42:08.250 --> 00:42:11.460
Now, this aspect of being a younger child

00:42:11.460 --> 00:42:14.310
really intrigues me.

00:42:14.310 --> 00:42:16.353
From Paridhi,

00:42:18.117 --> 00:42:22.247
@mythicanoi, "I'm two years
younger than my sister."

00:42:22.247 --> 00:42:23.687
"It's a bittersweet relationship."

00:42:23.687 --> 00:42:26.617
"One thing I hate about it
is all the competition."

00:42:26.617 --> 00:42:28.067
"All my grades are compared to hers."

00:42:28.067 --> 00:42:30.117
"All my talents are
compared to that of hers."

00:42:30.117 --> 00:42:31.767
"Living that way for 18 years,

00:42:31.767 --> 00:42:34.217
"I have learned to calculate my self-worth

00:42:34.217 --> 00:42:35.907
"from other people's achievements."

00:42:35.907 --> 00:42:38.207
"It's not about what I
like, or what I can do,

00:42:38.207 --> 00:42:41.417
"it's more about, am I
better than the other person

00:42:41.417 --> 00:42:44.150
"because that is the only way
my parents will accept me."

00:42:44.150 --> 00:42:44.983
- Hmm.

00:42:47.880 --> 00:42:50.160
- Wow, okay, so we've talked a lot

00:42:50.160 --> 00:42:52.484
about my competitive nature.

00:42:52.484 --> 00:42:56.760
We had one very infamous podcast

00:42:56.760 --> 00:42:57.610
where I tried to convince you

00:42:57.610 --> 00:42:59.186
that you were also competitive.

00:42:59.186 --> 00:43:00.250
(Link chuckles)

00:43:00.250 --> 00:43:02.760
- And I won that argument.
- Yeah.

00:43:02.760 --> 00:43:04.073
- So--
- That was a joke.

00:43:05.370 --> 00:43:09.620
- I am, and I think
another way to say it is,

00:43:09.620 --> 00:43:13.100
I definitely come from a
performance-based household, right?

00:43:13.100 --> 00:43:17.500
Now, I am incredibly
thankful and grateful for

00:43:17.500 --> 00:43:19.360
the way my parents raised me and I think

00:43:19.360 --> 00:43:21.210
I have so much to be thankful for.

00:43:21.210 --> 00:43:25.340
I do think that they had high
expectations for performance.

00:43:25.340 --> 00:43:28.390
It was like, when you
brought home the good grades

00:43:28.390 --> 00:43:31.000
or you excelled in a sport,

00:43:31.000 --> 00:43:33.803
you received praise for
those kinds of things.

00:43:35.870 --> 00:43:39.880
So I think that the way that
my personality interacted

00:43:39.880 --> 00:43:43.970
with that is, I was like, "Oh,
I see how this game works."

00:43:43.970 --> 00:43:46.180
And I turned a lot of things into a game

00:43:46.180 --> 00:43:49.430
and it's turned me into a
very performance-based person.

00:43:49.430 --> 00:43:52.430
I'm an Enneagram Three and so I kinda

00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:54.660
enter into every single arena of life

00:43:54.660 --> 00:43:56.530
thinking about how I can win, right?

00:43:56.530 --> 00:43:58.420
How I can come out on
top of the situation.

00:43:58.420 --> 00:44:00.190
Not necessarily at the
expense of other people,

00:44:00.190 --> 00:44:03.430
but just like, I want
to be good at this thing

00:44:03.430 --> 00:44:04.830
that I am now engaging with.

00:44:06.900 --> 00:44:08.920
I've never thought about it

00:44:08.920 --> 00:44:11.280
being anything to do with birth order,

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:14.425
or in comparison to my brother until now.

00:44:14.425 --> 00:44:15.258
(Rhett chuckles)

00:44:15.258 --> 00:44:17.979
Until this observation--
- So,

00:44:17.979 --> 00:44:22.160
do you recall, I mean comparing yourself?

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:23.870
He was older.

00:44:23.870 --> 00:44:26.090
I don't know if basketball
is a good example.

00:44:26.090 --> 00:44:31.090
I know that you guys both were
laser focused on basketball.

00:44:32.579 --> 00:44:34.703
He was on the high school team.

00:44:35.890 --> 00:44:38.160
You were on the JV team as a freshman,

00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:39.800
so you were never on the same squad.

00:44:39.800 --> 00:44:40.650
- One game.

00:44:40.650 --> 00:44:41.860
- One game?

00:44:41.860 --> 00:44:43.830
- State playoffs, my freshman year,

00:44:43.830 --> 00:44:46.577
they pulled me up to varsity
and we were on the court

00:44:46.577 --> 00:44:48.540
in the first round of the state playoffs

00:44:48.540 --> 00:44:50.811
for like a few plays.

00:44:50.811 --> 00:44:53.980
- And did you--
- Coach, just did it.

00:44:53.980 --> 00:44:55.700
I think he just did it
so it could be like,

00:44:55.700 --> 00:44:58.920
the McLaughlin boys are on
the floor at the same time.

00:44:58.920 --> 00:45:01.270
- You would you pass it
(indiscernible)

00:45:01.270 --> 00:45:02.470
to each other?

00:45:02.470 --> 00:45:03.560
I mean, I was a freshmen.

00:45:03.560 --> 00:45:05.733
I was like, I'd been on JV.

00:45:06.770 --> 00:45:10.700
I'd been on JV, not starting,
by the way, as a freshmen.

00:45:10.700 --> 00:45:13.870
- Oh, so you just, your pants?
- Because Coach Mace,

00:45:13.870 --> 00:45:17.430
in his great wisdom played
Jason Batman before me.

00:45:17.430 --> 00:45:18.490
- Oh, you're bitter.

00:45:18.490 --> 00:45:19.769
(Rhett guffaws)

00:45:19.769 --> 00:45:21.530
- I am a little bit bitter about that,

00:45:21.530 --> 00:45:23.277
but Coach Gage was
like, "I don't know what

00:45:23.277 --> 00:45:25.277
"Coach Mace doing, but
I know that you're good

00:45:25.277 --> 00:45:26.697
"and we're gonna bring you up to varsity

00:45:26.697 --> 00:45:28.600
"in your sophomore year," which he did,

00:45:28.600 --> 00:45:31.049
but he also brought me up
for the state playoffs.

00:45:31.049 --> 00:45:33.680
So, yeah, there was a--

00:45:33.680 --> 00:45:35.740
- You guys will be out in your front yard,

00:45:35.740 --> 00:45:36.650
on your basketball court,

00:45:36.650 --> 00:45:38.950
both doing drills.
- There was a whole lot

00:45:38.950 --> 00:45:40.990
of competitiveness between the two of us

00:45:40.990 --> 00:45:42.283
with whatever we were doing.
- Like playing one-on-one?

00:45:42.283 --> 00:45:44.470
- Playing, one-on-one, playing horse.

00:45:44.470 --> 00:45:49.470
Some of the most epic, long,
extended games of horse

00:45:50.740 --> 00:45:52.100
in the summertime.

00:45:52.100 --> 00:45:55.550
One-on-one games that would
often end with me crying

00:45:55.550 --> 00:45:57.150
and running inside because
I was the younger one

00:45:57.150 --> 00:45:59.910
and I was losing most almost all the time

00:45:59.910 --> 00:46:01.090
maybe till...

00:46:02.070 --> 00:46:03.580
In high school, every once in a while

00:46:03.580 --> 00:46:06.490
I might be able to win just
'cause I was getting so tall.

00:46:06.490 --> 00:46:10.241
- Uh, huh, how tall is Cole?

00:46:10.241 --> 00:46:11.074
- 6'2"?

00:46:12.770 --> 00:46:14.050
- Okay.
- 6.

00:46:14.050 --> 00:46:15.200
- Okay, yeah.

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:17.003
He might have me by a couple inches.

00:46:18.440 --> 00:46:20.230
- I think that...

00:46:20.230 --> 00:46:24.330
Here's the thing, I
think that he also might.

00:46:24.330 --> 00:46:26.020
I don't know if he knows
what Enneagram he is,

00:46:26.020 --> 00:46:28.113
but he's got a lot of, 3 in him.

00:46:29.380 --> 00:46:31.203
And so,

00:46:32.680 --> 00:46:36.290
I think that he's also an
achiever and a performer.

00:46:36.290 --> 00:46:39.310
And so I don't think,
again, I don't think it was,

00:46:39.310 --> 00:46:41.630
hey, I'm a performer because
I was a younger sibling.

00:46:41.630 --> 00:46:43.860
I think that maybe both of us are that way

00:46:43.860 --> 00:46:46.530
because there was, first of
all, kind of pre-wired to that

00:46:46.530 --> 00:46:50.430
and then growing up in a family
that kinda reinforced that.

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:54.563
So, we both kinda are task
and goal-oriented people.

00:46:56.830 --> 00:46:59.573
But there was a lot of comparison,

00:47:02.020 --> 00:47:02.857
but I don't remember,

00:47:02.857 --> 00:47:05.560
I don't remember--
- Like you didn't--

00:47:05.560 --> 00:47:07.257
- My parents saying things
like, "Well, your brother

00:47:07.257 --> 00:47:09.620
"did this so you need to do that."

00:47:09.620 --> 00:47:10.842
I don't remember,

00:47:10.842 --> 00:47:12.100
I don't remember that.

00:47:12.100 --> 00:47:16.090
You guys had a decent relationship.

00:47:16.090 --> 00:47:19.493
I mean, from my perspective,
coming over for sleepovers,

00:47:21.030 --> 00:47:23.350
I would interact with Cole too.

00:47:23.350 --> 00:47:24.730
We would listening to music.

00:47:24.730 --> 00:47:29.730
Yes, he would give me the LL Cool J tape

00:47:30.190 --> 00:47:32.140
and you would play the Young MC tape

00:47:32.140 --> 00:47:34.392
and you would both try to tell me who,

00:47:34.392 --> 00:47:37.030
try to argue which was the coolest

00:47:37.030 --> 00:47:38.820
and which made me feel awesome.

00:47:38.820 --> 00:47:41.470
I was like the swing vote here.

00:47:41.470 --> 00:47:43.090
But he wasn't--
- Was cooler?

00:47:43.090 --> 00:47:44.610
Young MC?

00:47:44.610 --> 00:47:46.710
- I think you had the Young MC tape, yeah.

00:47:47.690 --> 00:47:49.080
And he had a...

00:47:49.080 --> 00:47:50.550
Kool Moe Dee is cooler.

00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:51.580
- But you said LL Cool J.

00:47:51.580 --> 00:47:54.210
- And Kool Moe, he had both of those.

00:47:54.210 --> 00:47:59.040
You know, he had--
- Young MC is not cool.

00:47:59.040 --> 00:48:02.770
He was cool for a
moment, but no longevity.

00:48:02.770 --> 00:48:03.603
- Yeah.

00:48:03.603 --> 00:48:04.600
Your brother was right at the time.

00:48:04.600 --> 00:48:05.433
- Yeah.

00:48:05.433 --> 00:48:08.700
But my point is when I would
come over to a sleepover,

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:10.117
there will be interactions,
I would experience...

00:48:10.117 --> 00:48:12.100
You know, I would see you two interact.

00:48:12.100 --> 00:48:14.300
It's not like he wouldn't
have anything to do with me,

00:48:14.300 --> 00:48:17.220
or you, because we were so much younger.

00:48:17.220 --> 00:48:20.350
You guys had an active relationship.

00:48:20.350 --> 00:48:21.500
- Yeah.
- I mean,

00:48:21.500 --> 00:48:24.430
there was a friendship
component to it, right?

00:48:24.430 --> 00:48:26.400
You didn't hate each other.
(Link chuckles)

00:48:26.400 --> 00:48:27.430
- We got into a lot of fights.

00:48:27.430 --> 00:48:28.600
I would say that...

00:48:30.670 --> 00:48:31.503
I dunno.

00:48:31.503 --> 00:48:36.503
I don't think we were as
emotionally available to each other

00:48:36.910 --> 00:48:39.290
and reliant on each other, as I would say

00:48:39.290 --> 00:48:41.470
even Locke and Shepard are.

00:48:41.470 --> 00:48:44.810
And Locke and Shepard have a
much larger age difference,

00:48:44.810 --> 00:48:48.850
which can both exacerbate and help that

00:48:48.850 --> 00:48:50.520
sort of reliance on one another.

00:48:50.520 --> 00:48:54.068
I think in their case it has

00:48:54.068 --> 00:48:57.200
enhanced that emotional connection.

00:48:57.200 --> 00:49:01.620
But I think that, it wasn't
until we got to be adults,

00:49:01.620 --> 00:49:04.240
that we would maybe have
a conversation that was--

00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:05.120
- Heavy.

00:49:05.120 --> 00:49:08.310
- Deeper and kinda went
below the surface of like,

00:49:08.310 --> 00:49:09.720
are we talking.

00:49:09.720 --> 00:49:12.940
As kids, it was, let's
have a conversation about

00:49:12.940 --> 00:49:15.820
what we're doing and how
we're doing it better,

00:49:15.820 --> 00:49:16.920
how we could do it better.

00:49:16.920 --> 00:49:19.221
But we're not gonna have a
conversation about how we feel,

00:49:19.221 --> 00:49:20.517
(Rhett chuckles)
you know?

00:49:20.517 --> 00:49:25.220
But that was kinda also a
family thing for us too, right.

00:49:25.220 --> 00:49:29.820
We didn't talk a whole lot
about feelings in my family.

00:49:29.820 --> 00:49:32.790
It wasn't that feelings
were bad, it was just that,

00:49:32.790 --> 00:49:34.503
it wasn't something that...

00:49:34.503 --> 00:49:36.780
Talking about feelings was not valued.

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:38.990
- But you didn't.

00:49:38.990 --> 00:49:42.330
My perception is you
didn't have any buried

00:49:42.330 --> 00:49:45.800
feelings of animosity
towards your brother.

00:49:45.800 --> 00:49:46.633
- No.
- I mean,

00:49:46.633 --> 00:49:48.500
the fights that you would have as in...

00:49:49.610 --> 00:49:53.550
He would pick on you about
having a crush on somebody

00:49:53.550 --> 00:49:55.570
and then you would get really angry.

00:49:55.570 --> 00:49:58.260
And I remember him
running back into his room

00:49:58.260 --> 00:50:01.350
and you had a stick of
deodorant in your hand

00:50:01.350 --> 00:50:04.260
and you threw it at his door
- Yeah.

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:05.670
- That he just slammed in your face.

00:50:05.670 --> 00:50:07.940
And I think it made a hole in the door.

00:50:07.940 --> 00:50:10.040
- I think it may have dented it.

00:50:10.040 --> 00:50:11.547
Yeah, and I think--
- And I was there.

00:50:11.547 --> 00:50:12.750
- the thing about that is that--

00:50:12.750 --> 00:50:15.270
- I was like, I'm glad I'm an only child.

00:50:15.270 --> 00:50:16.860
- He would see--
- I don't wanna get it

00:50:16.860 --> 00:50:17.693
with deodorant.

00:50:17.693 --> 00:50:20.450
- He would see that he
could get you to join in

00:50:20.450 --> 00:50:22.260
and that would make me very mad.

00:50:22.260 --> 00:50:24.590
If he recruited my friends
- Embarrassing.

00:50:24.590 --> 00:50:26.010
to be a part of this thing.

00:50:26.010 --> 00:50:28.290
Like that's harder to with
than just him doing it.

00:50:28.290 --> 00:50:31.240
But he would also do this
thing where he would...

00:50:32.460 --> 00:50:34.460
No one really talked about this but...

00:50:36.200 --> 00:50:37.620
I've seen this with Locke and Shepard.

00:50:37.620 --> 00:50:40.257
Now this is an older, younger
thing I've seen quite a bit.

00:50:40.257 --> 00:50:42.940
He would talk about how it was gonna be

00:50:42.940 --> 00:50:46.570
so difficult for me when I
got to, fill-the-blank grade.

00:50:46.570 --> 00:50:49.621
He'd be like, "When you get to 4th grade,

00:50:49.621 --> 00:50:52.970
(Link guffaws)
"it's gonna rock your world."

00:50:52.970 --> 00:50:54.800
And then I would get to 4th
grade and it would be just

00:50:54.800 --> 00:50:55.850
as fun as 1st grade.

00:50:55.850 --> 00:50:58.400
I always loved school and never thought

00:50:58.400 --> 00:50:59.910
that it was difficult, right.

00:50:59.910 --> 00:51:02.800
And--
- So, it never worked?

00:51:02.800 --> 00:51:03.633
He never scared you?

00:51:03.633 --> 00:51:07.230
- Every time I would get to
the grade, it would be fine.

00:51:07.230 --> 00:51:10.080
And it became a...
(Link chuckles)

00:51:10.080 --> 00:51:11.980
It became this funny,

00:51:11.980 --> 00:51:15.610
like, recurring joke in my family.

00:51:15.610 --> 00:51:18.391
It was, "Well Rhett's in 7th grade now and

00:51:18.391 --> 00:51:21.120
"he says it's okay," you know?

00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:23.702
So, Cole definitely would
warn me about things.

00:51:23.702 --> 00:51:26.490
That would happen quite a bit.

00:51:26.490 --> 00:51:28.870
- I'm super-jealous of not
having an older brother.

00:51:28.870 --> 00:51:31.770
Like when it comes to music is one thing.

00:51:31.770 --> 00:51:33.810
That's why I brought that up, is that,

00:51:33.810 --> 00:51:35.110
or whatever the case.

00:51:35.110 --> 00:51:38.990
Whether it's like.

00:51:38.990 --> 00:51:41.720
My development was stunted, I believe.

00:51:41.720 --> 00:51:44.070
When you have an older brother,
like you're talking about.

00:51:44.070 --> 00:51:47.050
You're observing Cole with his girlfriend

00:51:47.050 --> 00:51:49.960
and figuring out, kinda between the lines

00:51:49.960 --> 00:51:51.160
what they're up to, you know?

00:51:51.160 --> 00:51:54.117
It might be, you know, you're
gaining confidence that,

00:51:54.117 --> 00:51:56.427
"I know this guy, I live with this guy.

00:51:56.427 --> 00:51:57.877
"This guy is ridiculous."

00:51:57.877 --> 00:52:00.670
"If he can have a girlfriend,
I can have a girlfriend."

00:52:00.670 --> 00:52:01.560
You know, it was like,

00:52:01.560 --> 00:52:05.200
I had so little confidence
to even have a girlfriend

00:52:05.200 --> 00:52:07.750
and I felt like I missed out on that.

00:52:07.750 --> 00:52:11.310
When it came to music,
it was just exposure to

00:52:11.310 --> 00:52:15.260
cool things that were above my head.

00:52:15.260 --> 00:52:18.300
Or movies, you know?

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:19.360
- Yeah, most of the kids--
- Everything--

00:52:19.360 --> 00:52:22.010
- who end up introducing you to

00:52:22.010 --> 00:52:24.000
the things that are for older kids,

00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:26.050
usually have an older brother.

00:52:26.050 --> 00:52:27.010
- Right.

00:52:27.010 --> 00:52:28.670
Like Lando watches...

00:52:30.474 --> 00:52:32.660
He's got a lot of stipulations

00:52:32.660 --> 00:52:35.733
about what he'll allow himself to enjoy.

00:52:35.733 --> 00:52:36.566
(Rhett chuckles)

00:52:36.566 --> 00:52:39.459
Again, it's self-imposed,
but for us, it's like,

00:52:39.459 --> 00:52:42.420
again we wanna watch things that we enjoy

00:52:42.420 --> 00:52:44.640
and he's just along for the ride.

00:52:44.640 --> 00:52:46.500
Whereas with the younger kids,

00:52:46.500 --> 00:52:48.640
when the first kids were younger,

00:52:48.640 --> 00:52:52.090
it was more just catering to
things that were age-specific.

00:52:52.090 --> 00:52:53.890
But by the time you get
to three, it's just like,

00:52:53.890 --> 00:52:57.100
hey, you're gonna be
stretched a little bit

00:52:57.100 --> 00:52:59.210
because there's other people pulling

00:53:00.969 --> 00:53:03.630
the median age of this whole group up

00:53:03.630 --> 00:53:04.620
so gotta adapt.

00:53:04.620 --> 00:53:06.860
- That not a birth order thing,
that's a personality thing

00:53:06.860 --> 00:53:10.730
because most, well in my
experience personally,

00:53:10.730 --> 00:53:13.030
in my experience with my
family and what I would say

00:53:13.030 --> 00:53:15.420
is my anecdotal evidence,

00:53:15.420 --> 00:53:20.420
most younger kids are excited
about getting to do the things

00:53:21.800 --> 00:53:25.680
that their older siblings
were not allowed to do.

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:28.300
Like the idea of getting
away with something,

00:53:28.300 --> 00:53:30.680
watching something that
you're not supposed to watch.

00:53:30.680 --> 00:53:32.060
Most kids are kinda like,

00:53:32.060 --> 00:53:35.540
they don't impose their own
standards like Lando does.

00:53:35.540 --> 00:53:38.930
And so, I just think, again,
that's not of birth order,

00:53:38.930 --> 00:53:40.160
family dynamic thing.

00:53:40.160 --> 00:53:44.270
I just think this is how these
particular kids are wired.

00:53:44.270 --> 00:53:46.461
- I wanted to bring up another dynamic.

00:53:46.461 --> 00:53:50.000
I love when we put out these prompts

00:53:50.000 --> 00:53:53.340
and then we get back something
that wasn't on our radar.

00:53:53.340 --> 00:53:55.630
And I'm not talking about if you're a twin

00:53:55.630 --> 00:53:58.000
because we've made an
executive decision that

00:53:58.000 --> 00:53:59.640
we're not talking about twins.

00:53:59.640 --> 00:54:01.340
They creep me out.
- Yeah.

00:54:01.340 --> 00:54:03.160
- And I don't wanna have nothing

00:54:03.160 --> 00:54:04.700
to do with 'em.
- Speaking of the devil.

00:54:04.700 --> 00:54:05.697
- So they are--
- Twins are the result

00:54:05.697 --> 00:54:07.080
of the devil's activity.
(Link chuckles)

00:54:07.080 --> 00:54:08.670
- You guys are out.

00:54:08.670 --> 00:54:09.748
- Triplets, whoa, don't get me

00:54:09.748 --> 00:54:10.581
started on that.
- No.

00:54:10.581 --> 00:54:12.640
Don't even say the word quadruplets.

00:54:12.640 --> 00:54:13.473
- Yeah.

00:54:13.473 --> 00:54:14.571
- Oh my gosh!

00:54:14.571 --> 00:54:17.270
But I thought this is great.

00:54:17.270 --> 00:54:18.863
I'll bundle these three together.

00:54:20.420 --> 00:54:23.430
If you have a sibling with special needs.

00:54:23.430 --> 00:54:25.747
Ashleigh tweeted, "I'm the youngest

00:54:25.747 --> 00:54:27.337
"with just an older brother."

00:54:27.337 --> 00:54:28.987
"My brother has autism."

00:54:28.987 --> 00:54:31.047
"It's weird being the youngest by age,

00:54:31.047 --> 00:54:34.267
"but being treated like the
oldest for most things."

00:54:34.267 --> 00:54:36.557
"I'm more of a parent
than a sister to him."

00:54:36.557 --> 00:54:38.237
"Love my brother to death though

00:54:38.237 --> 00:54:39.997
"and wouldn't change a thing."

00:54:40.870 --> 00:54:45.447
Sophia responded said,
"Being the younger sibling

00:54:45.447 --> 00:54:48.547
"of an autistic child
has been tough at times,

00:54:48.547 --> 00:54:52.127
"but it has also given me
compassion and patience

00:54:52.127 --> 00:54:55.090
"at the times I needed it the most."

00:54:55.090 --> 00:55:00.027
And then Caroline responded,
"Also younger sibling."

00:55:00.027 --> 00:55:03.097
"I'm the youngest, but one of
my older brothers is disabled

00:55:03.097 --> 00:55:06.187
"and has always needed
round the clock care.

00:55:06.187 --> 00:55:10.317
"I'm now a special school
teacher and have a passion

00:55:10.317 --> 00:55:13.030
"for helping disabled
students find purpose."

00:55:13.030 --> 00:55:15.560
I love the fact that
we got these responses

00:55:15.560 --> 00:55:17.920
to expand this conversation.

00:55:17.920 --> 00:55:21.470
It's interesting that all three
of them are younger siblings

00:55:21.470 --> 00:55:25.203
who have an older, special need sibling.

00:55:26.300 --> 00:55:28.470
Obviously that's not always the case,

00:55:28.470 --> 00:55:31.710
but I think it's a little
more unexpected that,

00:55:31.710 --> 00:55:33.560
okay, as a younger sibling,

00:55:33.560 --> 00:55:34.640
you're not getting away with more,

00:55:34.640 --> 00:55:39.065
you're having this older
sibling experience and it's...

00:55:39.065 --> 00:55:42.720
You know, being a part of
a family dynamic like this,

00:55:42.720 --> 00:55:47.720
you learn things that a lot of us

00:55:50.890 --> 00:55:53.240
never have, I will say,
the privilege of learning,

00:55:53.240 --> 00:55:54.140
expand who you are.

00:55:54.140 --> 00:55:56.697
I love when they're all saying,

00:55:56.697 --> 00:55:58.616
"I wouldn't change it for the world."

00:55:58.616 --> 00:56:00.927
"I've grown so much as a person."

00:56:00.927 --> 00:56:04.490
"I've accessed a level of
compassion and patience

00:56:04.490 --> 00:56:05.790
- Yeah.
- "that develops me."

00:56:05.790 --> 00:56:08.116
I mean, it's well, and
that's extremely difficult,

00:56:08.116 --> 00:56:09.290
but very cool.
- I don't have any

00:56:09.290 --> 00:56:12.713
personal experience with this,
but everyone that I know,

00:56:14.060 --> 00:56:16.523
who has a family member
with special needs,

00:56:19.210 --> 00:56:20.120
has

00:56:21.660 --> 00:56:25.740
this increased, sort of,
appreciation for life.

00:56:25.740 --> 00:56:27.630
And I don't mean like, oh, I'm so glad

00:56:27.630 --> 00:56:29.420
that I'm not in this situation, but no,

00:56:29.420 --> 00:56:31.780
like being in the
relationship with a person

00:56:31.780 --> 00:56:34.960
who has special needs has given
them a perspective on life

00:56:34.960 --> 00:56:38.560
that is deeper.

00:56:38.560 --> 00:56:39.393
And it's not, you know.

00:56:39.393 --> 00:56:40.770
You got Caroline saying that

00:56:40.770 --> 00:56:44.053
she is helping disabled
students find purpose.

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:49.460
I also think that disabled
people help us find purpose.

00:56:49.460 --> 00:56:50.760
- Hm.
- You know what I'm saying?

00:56:50.760 --> 00:56:54.860
I think that that is a really
common theme that you see

00:56:55.820 --> 00:56:57.920
in people who have
members in their families

00:56:57.920 --> 00:57:01.120
who have special needs is
that they have not been.

00:57:01.120 --> 00:57:05.250
Now, first of all, okay,
yes, in lots of cases,

00:57:05.250 --> 00:57:07.920
maybe there is a burden
aspect to it in that

00:57:07.920 --> 00:57:10.740
this is a lot of work,
this is a lot of attention.

00:57:10.740 --> 00:57:12.790
There's a lot of sacrifices
that are made by parents

00:57:12.790 --> 00:57:13.623
and by siblings,

00:57:13.623 --> 00:57:15.020
of course, that's an aspect.
- Sure.

00:57:15.020 --> 00:57:15.853
- I can only.

00:57:15.853 --> 00:57:18.933
I can't imagine because I
don't have that experience.

00:57:20.630 --> 00:57:23.460
But at the same time I think
that there's a huge blessing

00:57:23.460 --> 00:57:25.260
that comes with that and
I think you're seeing that

00:57:25.260 --> 00:57:28.260
in these in these mythical
beasts who shared.

00:57:28.260 --> 00:57:32.600
- As we shift to the only child responses,

00:57:32.600 --> 00:57:35.280
I'm just very, I'm just struck with

00:57:37.630 --> 00:57:41.210
how much you are shaped by
the environment you grew up in

00:57:41.210 --> 00:57:44.020
in terms of like, again, it's
not just about birth order,

00:57:44.020 --> 00:57:47.670
but it's about additional
personalities in your home.

00:57:47.670 --> 00:57:52.670
And the deeply intimate level
of exposure you have to people

00:57:54.030 --> 00:57:59.030
who are either adults or
developing kids at different ages.

00:58:00.870 --> 00:58:05.870
It's just, I mean, it
has to have an impact on

00:58:07.100 --> 00:58:08.835
how you develop,

00:58:08.835 --> 00:58:11.943
at least in the short-term
when you're in that situation,

00:58:12.950 --> 00:58:15.450
in comparison to being an only child.

00:58:15.450 --> 00:58:18.707
Being in people's mess, you know,

00:58:18.707 --> 00:58:20.457
(Link chuckles)
and understanding.

00:58:22.715 --> 00:58:25.622
You just really get to know people

00:58:25.622 --> 00:58:26.455
(Link chuckles)

00:58:26.455 --> 00:58:29.070
when you're related to
them and live with them

00:58:29.070 --> 00:58:30.963
and living on top of each other.

00:58:32.850 --> 00:58:37.850
And yeah, so I do feel like I missed out.

00:58:38.210 --> 00:58:42.067
Bri Shear tweeted, "Growing
up being an only child

00:58:42.067 --> 00:58:43.247
"was awful."

00:58:44.630 --> 00:58:45.463
Wow.

00:58:45.463 --> 00:58:46.296
"I was so lonely.

00:58:46.296 --> 00:58:49.247
"I always wanted a sibling and
constantly annoyed my parents

00:58:49.247 --> 00:58:50.710
"to give me one."

00:58:50.710 --> 00:58:51.810
(Link laughing)

00:58:51.810 --> 00:58:53.207
So, "Give me a sibling."

00:58:54.470 --> 00:58:57.100
Well, you know, there's
a process involved,

00:58:57.100 --> 00:58:58.250
- Yeah, or a stork.
- Bri.

00:58:58.250 --> 00:58:59.320
I have a--
- Did you want this?

00:58:59.320 --> 00:59:00.500
- I have a lot of...

00:59:00.500 --> 00:59:02.900
I don't recall ever asking for a sibling.

00:59:02.900 --> 00:59:05.120
I mean, you know, my mom...

00:59:05.120 --> 00:59:07.030
Yeah, 'cause I had the half-sister

00:59:07.030 --> 00:59:08.940
and then there was the
divorce and it was just like,

00:59:08.940 --> 00:59:11.730
well, it's like, okay mom is single.

00:59:11.730 --> 00:59:13.660
It's just the two of us.

00:59:13.660 --> 00:59:15.400
It just wasn't an environment to

00:59:17.230 --> 00:59:18.880
hope for a sibling.

00:59:18.880 --> 00:59:20.742
It didn't seem like that was something

00:59:20.742 --> 00:59:21.575
that was gonna happen.

00:59:21.575 --> 00:59:22.930
And there's also some--
- Maybe there are

00:59:22.930 --> 00:59:23.763
some kids that would have been like,

00:59:23.763 --> 00:59:25.287
"Aww, I hope she gets married
and then I'll have like...

00:59:25.287 --> 00:59:26.370
"There'll be a baby."

00:59:26.370 --> 00:59:28.040
Did you ever think?

00:59:28.040 --> 00:59:29.060
- I don't think so.

00:59:29.060 --> 00:59:31.020
Yeah, I don't think so.
- Did you ever hang out

00:59:31.020 --> 00:59:32.090
with babies

00:59:33.060 --> 00:59:36.210
and think, "Oh I would like
one of these in my house?"

00:59:36.210 --> 00:59:37.260
- She had a...

00:59:37.260 --> 00:59:42.260
Her friend, Donna, I
remember had this cute

00:59:42.370 --> 00:59:44.230
little toddler daughter.

00:59:44.230 --> 00:59:47.980
And I remember being infatuated with her

00:59:47.980 --> 00:59:51.820
in a younger sister kind of
way, like holding the baby,

00:59:51.820 --> 00:59:53.893
or playing with the toddler.

00:59:56.059 --> 00:59:58.983
I mean, I had a few baby dolls myself.

01:00:00.760 --> 01:00:02.110
And I don't know if that was some sort.

01:00:02.110 --> 01:00:03.070
I think there was a.

01:00:03.070 --> 01:00:05.720
It was more of a,

01:00:05.720 --> 01:00:09.940
it's so, you know, these
babies are so cute,

01:00:09.940 --> 01:00:12.330
but maybe there was a loneliness thing.

01:00:12.330 --> 01:00:14.860
I definitely remember being in my room,

01:00:14.860 --> 01:00:17.970
even when I had the step-sister.

01:00:17.970 --> 01:00:19.970
That age difference was such that

01:00:19.970 --> 01:00:22.960
we really didn't have a relationship.

01:00:22.960 --> 01:00:26.470
I would be in my room a
lot, listening to the radio,

01:00:26.470 --> 01:00:28.900
or playing with my G.I. Joes, you know,

01:00:28.900 --> 01:00:30.190
I've told this story.

01:00:30.190 --> 01:00:32.730
I didn't listen to the radio,
or music for the longest time

01:00:32.730 --> 01:00:35.960
because my stepsister liked music.

01:00:35.960 --> 01:00:37.167
- Yeah.
- I just wanted to assert--

01:00:37.167 --> 01:00:38.720
- You gotta differentiate yourself--

01:00:38.720 --> 01:00:40.230
- myself.

01:00:40.230 --> 01:00:43.190
But yeah and I was certainly
lonely and I had this...

01:00:46.540 --> 01:00:48.020
Sav tweeted at us.

01:00:48.020 --> 01:00:50.727
S-A-V, siccvanna,

01:00:50.727 --> 01:00:52.970
"As an only child from Harnett
County with a single mom,"

01:00:52.970 --> 01:00:53.803
What!
- What!

01:00:53.803 --> 01:00:55.157
- Just like me.

01:00:55.157 --> 01:00:57.447
"I felt like I was
socially behind my peers."

01:00:57.447 --> 01:01:00.247
"It wasn't until college
that I found and developed

01:01:00.247 --> 01:01:03.647
"a personality and opinions
that were wholly my own."

01:01:04.577 --> 01:01:06.977
"I struggle with boundaries of oversharing

01:01:06.977 --> 01:01:08.207
"and self isolation."

01:01:08.207 --> 01:01:11.767
"I often resent not having
a sibling to confide in

01:01:11.767 --> 01:01:14.097
"who could understand exactly
where I'm coming from,

01:01:14.097 --> 01:01:16.717
"without the judgment
that a parent might have."

01:01:16.717 --> 01:01:18.971
"Even when I know my parents
want the best for me,

01:01:18.971 --> 01:01:20.527
"it's still a lot of
pressure and responsibility

01:01:20.527 --> 01:01:22.510
"to be a, 'Good daughter.'"

01:01:22.510 --> 01:01:27.510
So, I mean, I relate to
being socially stunted.

01:01:27.980 --> 01:01:29.210
I was...

01:01:29.210 --> 01:01:31.910
You had a voracious
appetite to spend the night

01:01:31.910 --> 01:01:36.145
at other kids' homes (Link
chuckles) of any age.

01:01:36.145 --> 01:01:37.030
(Link chuckles)
- Yeah.

01:01:37.030 --> 01:01:38.950
- And that's so strange to me.
- Insatiable.

01:01:38.950 --> 01:01:43.950
- I mean, as close as we became,
I know I was very anxious

01:01:44.410 --> 01:01:48.667
to go spend the night at your
house or, other kids' homes

01:01:48.667 --> 01:01:50.340
and I rarely did it.

01:01:50.340 --> 01:01:51.500
I just didn't.

01:01:51.500 --> 01:01:54.570
And then whenever I would go
over and we would interact

01:01:54.570 --> 01:01:56.570
with the other kids in the neighborhood,

01:01:58.200 --> 01:02:03.150
it was a high-anxiety situation
for me, looking back on it.

01:02:03.150 --> 01:02:04.210
I just wasn't.

01:02:04.210 --> 01:02:06.480
I wasn't used to being around

01:02:06.480 --> 01:02:10.400
a bunch of boisterous, assertive kids.

01:02:10.400 --> 01:02:12.450
I definitely remember in preschool,

01:02:12.450 --> 01:02:15.030
being afraid of all the other boys

01:02:15.030 --> 01:02:16.120
and I would hang out with the girls

01:02:16.120 --> 01:02:17.795
because at least they were chilled out.

01:02:17.795 --> 01:02:22.795
- But you also went to preschool,
kinda daycare situation

01:02:23.570 --> 01:02:24.800
earlier than I did.

01:02:24.800 --> 01:02:25.733
- I did, yeah.

01:02:26.660 --> 01:02:30.250
I did preschool out here
actually, in Thousand Oaks

01:02:30.250 --> 01:02:31.360
was the first.

01:02:31.360 --> 01:02:35.330
But yeah, it's funny because I actually

01:02:35.330 --> 01:02:38.829
am more on the introverted
side as an adult.

01:02:38.829 --> 01:02:43.510
I'm fine with large groups of
people and I can adapt to it

01:02:43.510 --> 01:02:47.510
and I don't have a lot
of anxiety, it's just,

01:02:47.510 --> 01:02:48.470
it's not my preference.

01:02:48.470 --> 01:02:51.610
My preference is to be alone, right.

01:02:51.610 --> 01:02:54.260
But it's weird that as a kid,

01:02:54.260 --> 01:02:56.962
my preference was very much like...

01:02:56.962 --> 01:02:58.980
It's almost like I got
it all out of the way.

01:02:58.980 --> 01:03:00.510
It was like--
- Oh, and you know,

01:03:00.510 --> 01:03:01.620
and we changed over time

01:03:01.620 --> 01:03:02.850
in that way too.
- Yeah, and so--

01:03:02.850 --> 01:03:04.270
- It's not locked in, that's for sure.

01:03:04.270 --> 01:03:07.871
- 'Cause I actually consider you, I mean,

01:03:07.871 --> 01:03:09.220
I don't know.

01:03:09.220 --> 01:03:13.010
You're kinda borderline
introvert, extrovert, I think.

01:03:13.010 --> 01:03:15.570
- I'm trying to figure
out how much this pandemic

01:03:15.570 --> 01:03:17.990
has influenced that about me

01:03:17.990 --> 01:03:22.990
because I'm noticing that I
really have to make up my mind

01:03:23.290 --> 01:03:27.500
to get out of my house and my comfort zone

01:03:27.500 --> 01:03:31.132
and my very small plans.

01:03:31.132 --> 01:03:34.700
So, I'm in a transition
period of getting back out

01:03:34.700 --> 01:03:36.533
into the world, as a lot of us are.

01:03:38.910 --> 01:03:41.103
Back in the day, as a kid,

01:03:43.290 --> 01:03:46.760
I caught up, but I had a
lot of catching up to do

01:03:46.760 --> 01:03:48.993
by high school.

01:03:49.900 --> 01:03:50.790
- And we've talked about this before,

01:03:50.790 --> 01:03:54.310
but I never perceived any of this.

01:03:54.310 --> 01:03:58.580
I did not have any perception
that you were uncomfortable.

01:03:58.580 --> 01:04:02.140
I mean, okay, yeah, we
went to Camp Caraway

01:04:02.140 --> 01:04:03.100
and you didn't take a dump

01:04:03.100 --> 01:04:04.320
the entire week,
(Link chuckles)

01:04:04.320 --> 01:04:05.503
but I was just like,

01:04:06.480 --> 01:04:07.663
okay, I mean, it happens.

01:04:07.663 --> 01:04:10.112
(Link chuckles)
- Sometimes doesn't happen,

01:04:10.112 --> 01:04:11.150
(both laugh)

01:04:11.150 --> 01:04:12.500
right?

01:04:12.500 --> 01:04:14.620
But I don't remember thinking, I mean,

01:04:14.620 --> 01:04:17.730
maybe my mom was like, "You
know, Link is having a,"

01:04:17.730 --> 01:04:20.127
she never told me, at
least I don't remember,

01:04:20.127 --> 01:04:22.247
"Link is really nervous about this week

01:04:22.247 --> 01:04:23.957
"and you need to help him."

01:04:23.957 --> 01:04:26.510
- Right.
- I didn't have any idea

01:04:26.510 --> 01:04:29.720
that you were not having a
great time that I was having.

01:04:29.720 --> 01:04:32.417
Every single thing that we
were doing, I was just like,

01:04:32.417 --> 01:04:34.580
"Everything about this is awesome."

01:04:34.580 --> 01:04:37.470
I tend to think that about
everything that I'm experiencing.

01:04:37.470 --> 01:04:39.177
I tend to think that I inflate it

01:04:39.177 --> 01:04:41.950
and make it even better
than it actually is

01:04:42.900 --> 01:04:45.880
before and after and during.

01:04:45.880 --> 01:04:47.440
I don't know where that comes from,

01:04:47.440 --> 01:04:50.350
but I never had any indication that

01:04:50.350 --> 01:04:52.990
you weren't just having as
great of a time as I was having.

01:04:52.990 --> 01:04:56.450
- Yeah, for me, I mean, if I
try to get specific to like,

01:04:56.450 --> 01:04:59.850
if you have an older sibling
who's gonna hold you down

01:04:59.850 --> 01:05:02.190
and drip spit from their mouth

01:05:02.190 --> 01:05:04.700
and dangle it onto your face--

01:05:04.700 --> 01:05:06.010
- Never hit my face

01:05:06.010 --> 01:05:06.843
- Again, right.

01:05:06.843 --> 01:05:07.676
- Had a lot of control.

01:05:07.676 --> 01:05:09.230
- It never hit your face, so
you're learning, it's like,

01:05:09.230 --> 01:05:12.530
this guys all bark, no bite,

01:05:12.530 --> 01:05:13.658
but for some--
- We never punched each other

01:05:13.658 --> 01:05:14.940
in the face.

01:05:14.940 --> 01:05:16.430
- You never punched
each other in the face?

01:05:16.430 --> 01:05:18.070
- We did the old Charley horse.

01:05:18.070 --> 01:05:19.010
That was our go-to.

01:05:19.010 --> 01:05:23.710
- But for me, you know, I
had to learn this out in the,

01:05:23.710 --> 01:05:26.140
with acquaintances.

01:05:26.140 --> 01:05:28.910
It's like, this person is boisterous.

01:05:28.910 --> 01:05:30.860
I don't know what they're up against.

01:05:30.860 --> 01:05:32.790
Does this kid have a knife?

01:05:32.790 --> 01:05:35.960
None of the kids had knives, but it...

01:05:35.960 --> 01:05:38.000
And I don't recall fixating on knives,

01:05:38.000 --> 01:05:40.120
but I'm just using an example
(Link chuckles) of like,

01:05:40.120 --> 01:05:43.477
you know, I had to assess
these things and come to these

01:05:43.477 --> 01:05:48.050
experience, how people
put on certain ways.

01:05:48.050 --> 01:05:51.780
And that doesn't necessarily
mean that they can back it up,

01:05:51.780 --> 01:05:54.480
or that that's the complete
picture of who they are.

01:05:54.480 --> 01:05:56.290
The more people you live
with and grow up with,

01:05:56.290 --> 01:05:57.910
the more you realize
we're all full of shit

01:05:57.910 --> 01:06:01.600
and we're all, you know,
we're all putting on our masks

01:06:01.600 --> 01:06:03.860
and protecting ourselves.

01:06:03.860 --> 01:06:06.880
'Cause you can see through
that in your own home.

01:06:06.880 --> 01:06:11.010
I just have one person in my
home for most of my growing up.

01:06:11.010 --> 01:06:13.871
So, I just had that data point.

01:06:13.871 --> 01:06:15.440
- But that might
- But the science says

01:06:15.440 --> 01:06:17.521
that you catch up and you're okay.

01:06:17.521 --> 01:06:20.120
- I just don't know how much, I mean,

01:06:20.120 --> 01:06:23.270
I think that the whole, I
definitely think that the whole

01:06:23.270 --> 01:06:24.867
feeling like you're behind and like,

01:06:24.867 --> 01:06:26.487
"Oh, I'm hearing this for the first time,

01:06:26.487 --> 01:06:29.027
"because I didn't have a
older brother older sister

01:06:29.027 --> 01:06:32.350
"to tell me," that completely makes sense.

01:06:32.350 --> 01:06:37.350
But the anxiety, or, "I
don't trust this person,"

01:06:39.290 --> 01:06:40.790
I tend to think that it's

01:06:40.790 --> 01:06:42.050
just personality
- Personality.

01:06:42.050 --> 01:06:43.010
- Sure, sure.

01:06:43.010 --> 01:06:44.280
It has a lot to do with that.

01:06:44.280 --> 01:06:47.090
I mean, I wonder if my kids asked me

01:06:48.437 --> 01:06:50.200
"How many kids I should have?"

01:06:50.200 --> 01:06:53.810
I mean, you know, we're in
such a different world now,

01:06:53.810 --> 01:06:58.810
but I think my inclination,
based on my experience

01:06:59.110 --> 01:07:01.567
is to not...

01:07:01.567 --> 01:07:05.970
And I'll even make a joke
with people, sometimes.

01:07:05.970 --> 01:07:07.423
It goes something like this,

01:07:08.457 --> 01:07:10.447
"Congratulations on the new baby."

01:07:12.347 --> 01:07:15.467
"Go ahead and have another
one 'cause you don't wanna...

01:07:15.467 --> 01:07:19.426
"Only child, you don't
wanna do that to your kids."

01:07:19.426 --> 01:07:20.259
And it's not really joking.

01:07:20.259 --> 01:07:21.092
- And then they're like,

01:07:21.092 --> 01:07:22.417
"Well, that's what we
were planning on doing."

01:07:22.417 --> 01:07:24.360
"We're in Los Angeles and
we just want one child."

01:07:24.360 --> 01:07:25.193
- I know.

01:07:25.193 --> 01:07:26.940
I try not to tell people
how many kids to have

01:07:26.940 --> 01:07:28.740
unless they ask me and no one ever ask me,

01:07:28.740 --> 01:07:30.680
so I end up--
- So you just tell 'em anyway.

01:07:30.680 --> 01:07:33.797
If I'm pressed to give advice, it would be

01:07:33.797 --> 01:07:35.290
"Don't just have one kid."

01:07:35.290 --> 01:07:38.030
It's better, it's better on everybody

01:07:38.030 --> 01:07:40.250
to have a second one except
for the parents, I think.

01:07:40.250 --> 01:07:42.650
- So you either don't have children,

01:07:42.650 --> 01:07:45.450
or if you're gonna have
children, have two kids?

01:07:45.450 --> 01:07:46.700
- Yeah.

01:07:46.700 --> 01:07:47.533
- Or three?

01:07:48.670 --> 01:07:50.220
- I'm happy that we have three.

01:07:51.490 --> 01:07:54.313
I can't imagine having four.

01:07:55.270 --> 01:07:56.440
I mean, like,

01:07:56.440 --> 01:07:59.980
he really slipped under
the wire there, you know.

01:07:59.980 --> 01:08:02.730
Barely got through and I'm glad he did,

01:08:02.730 --> 01:08:06.040
but there's never ever a point in my mind

01:08:06.040 --> 01:08:07.410
that I wish I had a fourth kid.

01:08:07.410 --> 01:08:08.243
- Well, there are--
- Never--

01:08:08.243 --> 01:08:10.770
- There are times--
- Chrissy sometimes seems like

01:08:10.770 --> 01:08:14.007
she wants a baby again and
I'm like, (Link screams)

01:08:14.007 --> 01:08:15.230
I like have this

01:08:16.136 --> 01:08:16.990
reaction.
- Well, it's funny

01:08:16.990 --> 01:08:18.323
- 'cause Jesse,

01:08:19.750 --> 01:08:20.683
I would say,

01:08:22.180 --> 01:08:23.973
multiple times a year,

01:08:27.020 --> 01:08:30.373
maybe she'll be a day late and then,

01:08:32.645 --> 01:08:34.595
she'll be like, "What if I'm pregnant?"

01:08:36.370 --> 01:08:40.650
She imagines what it would
feel like to be pregnant.

01:08:40.650 --> 01:08:44.490
And thus far it is--
- Imagine--

01:08:44.490 --> 01:08:45.750
- It's definitely seeming
like she's staring

01:08:45.750 --> 01:08:48.020
into an abyss of doom

01:08:48.020 --> 01:08:48.893
when she thinks about it.
- Well, you can't help her

01:08:48.893 --> 01:08:50.000
with it.

01:08:50.000 --> 01:08:51.820
Imagining what it would
be like to be pregnant

01:08:51.820 --> 01:08:56.820
is a good exercise
because that is not a...

01:08:56.880 --> 01:08:58.780
That's a tough experience.

01:08:58.780 --> 01:09:01.310
Having another child, or having a baby

01:09:02.490 --> 01:09:05.243
is a thing that somehow
some people still think

01:09:05.243 --> 01:09:07.930
is a great idea.
- Well, so there's definitely

01:09:07.930 --> 01:09:11.070
nothing about, there's nothing from Jessie

01:09:11.070 --> 01:09:13.100
where she's excited about it.

01:09:13.100 --> 01:09:18.100
And again, I'm very, very unlikely

01:09:18.590 --> 01:09:20.270
to make my wife pregnant at this point

01:09:20.270 --> 01:09:22.197
having had a vasectomy.

01:09:22.197 --> 01:09:23.030
(Link chuckles)
- Not impossible,

01:09:23.030 --> 01:09:24.600
but very unlikely, right?

01:09:24.600 --> 01:09:28.120
So for me, it all just
boils down to the fact

01:09:28.120 --> 01:09:30.337
that I've always wanted a daughter

01:09:30.337 --> 01:09:32.680
and my only daughter is Barbara.

01:09:32.680 --> 01:09:34.550
And I treat her like a little girl

01:09:34.550 --> 01:09:36.900
and I talk to her about
all that she can achieve

01:09:36.900 --> 01:09:39.135
in anything that she wants and, you know,

01:09:39.135 --> 01:09:40.250
(Link chuckles)
girl power.

01:09:40.250 --> 01:09:44.262
All my girl power sort of
encouragement goes to my dog.

01:09:44.262 --> 01:09:48.000
And that's kind of cool in one sense

01:09:48.000 --> 01:09:49.650
but it's a little bit sad because

01:09:51.120 --> 01:09:52.450
Barbara gonna die at some point.

01:09:52.450 --> 01:09:53.650
Before she gets to be the age

01:09:53.650 --> 01:09:56.420
where she would go off to
college, she's gonna die.

01:09:56.420 --> 01:09:59.360
- Yeah, I'm glad that we experienced

01:10:00.783 --> 01:10:02.960
a daughter and sons.

01:10:02.960 --> 01:10:05.630
And that dynamic, having a daughter first

01:10:05.630 --> 01:10:08.170
and like the whole
bossiness we talked about,

01:10:08.170 --> 01:10:10.610
I think that really played
into her personality

01:10:10.610 --> 01:10:13.910
and stuff like that as
opposed to having a,

01:10:13.910 --> 01:10:15.970
if Lincoln was first and
Lily was in the middle.

01:10:15.970 --> 01:10:16.843
Who knows?

01:10:16.843 --> 01:10:18.913
Everything will be different, maybe.

01:10:19.810 --> 01:10:20.643
So I think it--
- Can I have the last word?-

01:10:20.643 --> 01:10:22.830
- It all has an impact,
but then it doesn't have

01:10:22.830 --> 01:10:25.400
this lasting, like deterministic impact.

01:10:25.400 --> 01:10:28.340
- Well, speaking of twins,
we can close with this.

01:10:31.129 --> 01:10:32.720
(Rhett chuckles)

01:10:32.720 --> 01:10:35.750
This is where twins are
actually really helpful, right.

01:10:35.750 --> 01:10:39.550
Is, and I'm not talking about
like planned twin experiments

01:10:39.550 --> 01:10:43.240
but there are some sort of
just circumstantial experience

01:10:43.240 --> 01:10:46.100
experiments where twins grow
up in different environments

01:10:46.100 --> 01:10:48.020
for they got separated
at birth or whatever.

01:10:48.020 --> 01:10:48.853
- Hm mm?

01:10:51.530 --> 01:10:53.570
- I don't know the results of those.

01:10:53.570 --> 01:10:54.870
So I'm not going to speak up about it.

01:10:54.870 --> 01:10:58.890
But that is the only way to
know what you just talked about

01:10:58.890 --> 01:11:00.420
which is like, well,
what would Lily be like

01:11:00.420 --> 01:11:02.193
if she wasn't the oldest child,

01:11:04.740 --> 01:11:07.370
is if she had a twin that
grew up in a different place

01:11:07.370 --> 01:11:09.190
or better yet a triplet or a quadruplet.

01:11:09.190 --> 01:11:11.094
And they all grew up in different places.

01:11:11.094 --> 01:11:14.143
- Hope nobody comes out of the woodwork.

01:11:16.090 --> 01:11:19.967
- I mean, the author of the
book, "The Sibling Effect,

01:11:19.967 --> 01:11:23.300
"What the Bonds Among Brothers
and Sisters Reveal About Us,"

01:11:23.300 --> 01:11:27.900
Jeffrey Kluger, said for plenty
of us, the only ones left

01:11:27.900 --> 01:11:30.890
at the end of the dance will
be the ones who brung us.

01:11:30.890 --> 01:11:32.540
The brothers and sisters who have been

01:11:32.540 --> 01:11:35.500
with us the longest loved us the hardest

01:11:35.500 --> 01:11:39.440
and by a wide margin, know us the best.

01:11:39.440 --> 01:11:42.300
So if you, if you've got
a sibling that you love

01:11:43.890 --> 01:11:46.740
send them some love
today, and if you don't

01:11:46.740 --> 01:11:48.550
we'll send you some love right now.

01:11:48.550 --> 01:11:51.920
- I do think that that
that's a good point.

01:11:51.920 --> 01:11:54.260
It's one thing to have
parents who know you

01:11:54.260 --> 01:11:56.540
and, you know, when you're like us,

01:11:56.540 --> 01:12:01.540
and you've lived sort of
a weird unexpected life

01:12:01.840 --> 01:12:04.400
and being able to be
successful in a medium

01:12:04.400 --> 01:12:07.390
where we become these public figures

01:12:07.390 --> 01:12:11.105
that more people know us
than we know people, right?

01:12:11.105 --> 01:12:14.020
Because of our faces being out there

01:12:15.130 --> 01:12:18.980
and almost everyone who has
a perception of who we are

01:12:18.980 --> 01:12:21.420
has an incorrect perception of who we are.

01:12:21.420 --> 01:12:22.740
People--
- Incomplete--

01:12:22.740 --> 01:12:24.840
- Like, or don't like, whatever.

01:12:24.840 --> 01:12:27.180
Their perception of us is a projection

01:12:27.180 --> 01:12:29.880
that we have chosen to curate
and put on the internet.

01:12:29.880 --> 01:12:31.650
Now it's pretty personal.

01:12:31.650 --> 01:12:34.330
It's pretty real, but
it is a curation, right?

01:12:34.330 --> 01:12:39.210
If you're a fan of us, you
like us, you don't like

01:12:39.210 --> 01:12:41.990
who we are and who we would
be if you were to live with us

01:12:41.990 --> 01:12:43.780
or if you were to have grown up with us.

01:12:43.780 --> 01:12:48.780
Now having parents who
can always ground you

01:12:48.900 --> 01:12:50.260
and always bring you back down

01:12:50.260 --> 01:12:54.608
to who you really are, is one thing.

01:12:54.608 --> 01:12:57.300
It is another thing to have a sibling,

01:12:57.300 --> 01:13:00.090
to have someone who is in your generation,

01:13:00.090 --> 01:13:02.440
who knows exactly who you are

01:13:02.440 --> 01:13:04.533
and sees you for exactly who you are.

01:13:05.610 --> 01:13:06.580
And I think that that's something

01:13:06.580 --> 01:13:09.370
that my brother can do fairly well

01:13:10.540 --> 01:13:14.280
is when I have a conversation with him...

01:13:15.326 --> 01:13:17.870
Having a conversation
with him is by default

01:13:17.870 --> 01:13:19.863
a grounding experience, right?

01:13:20.930 --> 01:13:21.890
He's not trying to ground me.

01:13:21.890 --> 01:13:23.280
He's not trying to bring me down--

01:13:23.280 --> 01:13:24.113
- Right.
- But you just,

01:13:24.113 --> 01:13:27.250
when you've got somebody who knows you

01:13:27.250 --> 01:13:31.040
apart from the curated public image,

01:13:31.040 --> 01:13:32.470
and this could be good friends, too.

01:13:32.470 --> 01:13:34.660
I mean, it's one of
the reasons that people

01:13:34.660 --> 01:13:39.660
who are like super-famous
celebrities who have created

01:13:40.060 --> 01:13:42.190
sort of cocoon of
people-pleasers around them,

01:13:42.190 --> 01:13:44.390
tend to be these narcissistic monsters.

01:13:44.390 --> 01:13:45.810
- Hmm.

01:13:45.810 --> 01:13:49.593
- It's people who,
regardless of what level of,

01:13:50.490 --> 01:13:53.263
society, they have
ascended to, quote unquote,

01:13:54.100 --> 01:13:57.870
have people who know exactly who they are

01:13:57.870 --> 01:14:00.460
and will tell them
exactly what they think.

01:14:00.460 --> 01:14:01.517
That's a healthy thing.

01:14:01.517 --> 01:14:05.630
And I think that siblings,
you need a sibling

01:14:05.630 --> 01:14:08.480
because of that, I guess you just got me.

01:14:08.480 --> 01:14:09.980
- Yeah, you're the closest thing I got.

01:14:09.980 --> 01:14:12.790
And we use the marriage analogy more

01:14:12.790 --> 01:14:16.667
than the sibling analogy
because when there's no blood,

01:14:20.340 --> 01:14:21.970
then if you can end at any moment.

01:14:21.970 --> 01:14:23.200
- Will there be blood?

01:14:23.200 --> 01:14:25.670
- We're not blood related,
we will never be blood.

01:14:25.670 --> 01:14:27.923
- If we punch each other in
the face, there might be.

01:14:30.090 --> 01:14:31.516
- Okay, you can deflect it at this point,

01:14:31.516 --> 01:14:32.400
(Rhett chuckling)
but I'm saying, yeah,

01:14:32.400 --> 01:14:35.040
there is something different
in our relationship

01:14:35.040 --> 01:14:37.490
than you have with your
brother, because of that,

01:14:42.745 --> 01:14:44.470
the way the bond was determined,

01:14:44.470 --> 01:14:47.210
like there's something
to the blood of it all.

01:14:47.210 --> 01:14:49.990
- For sure.
- It's immutable.

01:14:49.990 --> 01:14:50.823
- Yeah.

01:14:52.130 --> 01:14:54.817
- And plus, he's not in your, you know,

01:14:54.817 --> 01:14:57.490
you're not working with
them every single day.

01:14:57.490 --> 01:15:02.180
And, we're not just friends
where you got this business

01:15:02.180 --> 01:15:04.430
that we're, you know, and this brand

01:15:04.430 --> 01:15:06.580
and everything that
we're creating together

01:15:07.790 --> 01:15:11.300
is an extremely connecting
and complicating factor

01:15:11.300 --> 01:15:12.860
in this conversation.

01:15:12.860 --> 01:15:17.730
But that's why you have
something with Cole

01:15:17.730 --> 01:15:19.430
and those conversations that, yeah,

01:15:19.430 --> 01:15:22.330
I guess I don't have anybody in that sense

01:15:22.330 --> 01:15:23.860
and you're not it.
- Hmm.

01:15:23.860 --> 01:15:24.910
- I just don't have it.

01:15:24.910 --> 01:15:29.260
- Maybe you can get like a
brother, a brother for hire.

01:15:29.260 --> 01:15:30.093
- I'm okay.

01:15:30.093 --> 01:15:31.950
I'm good enough, you know...

01:15:31.950 --> 01:15:34.090
- You can't recreate that anyway.

01:15:34.090 --> 01:15:37.920
- I would just rather get my
way more often in other senses.

01:15:37.920 --> 01:15:38.827
- Okay, well--

01:15:39.953 --> 01:15:41.625
(crosstalk)

01:15:41.625 --> 01:15:45.810
- Just to play the--
- The only child card.

01:15:45.810 --> 01:15:49.630
- So you've got a brother,
I've got the only child card.

01:15:49.630 --> 01:15:50.960
Okay, it all evens out.

01:15:50.960 --> 01:15:52.540
- Let us know what you think.

01:15:52.540 --> 01:15:54.480
Thank you for those of
the hundreds of you,

01:15:54.480 --> 01:15:59.480
who who interacted with
us on Twitter and, um...

01:15:59.890 --> 01:16:00.723
- You got a wreck?

01:16:00.723 --> 01:16:01.930
- We'll keep the conversation going.

01:16:01.930 --> 01:16:04.210
In the meantime, I do
have a wreck and effect,

01:16:04.210 --> 01:16:06.850
complete left turn,
unrelated to everything

01:16:06.850 --> 01:16:07.683
that we're talking about.

01:16:07.683 --> 01:16:09.190
Just a book that I'm reading.

01:16:09.190 --> 01:16:12.040
It is actually super-relevant right now.

01:16:12.040 --> 01:16:15.200
I found myself wanting to read this book,

01:16:15.200 --> 01:16:17.717
because one of the
interesting thing that things

01:16:17.717 --> 01:16:19.930
that has happened as
we're kind of coming out

01:16:19.930 --> 01:16:23.817
of the pandemic and things
are getting back to normal is,

01:16:23.817 --> 01:16:25.710
"Oh, what do you know?"

01:16:25.710 --> 01:16:27.510
Mass shootings are a thing again--

01:16:27.510 --> 01:16:28.900
- Hmm...
- In America, right?

01:16:28.900 --> 01:16:31.320
Sadly, tragically.

01:16:31.320 --> 01:16:36.060
And there's all this debate
about the Second Amendment.

01:16:36.060 --> 01:16:38.107
And I just found myself thinking,

01:16:38.107 --> 01:16:40.187
"You know, I don't really..."

01:16:40.187 --> 01:16:42.347
"I hear people say things
about the Second Amendment

01:16:42.347 --> 01:16:45.877
"and I either didn't pay
attention when we talked

01:16:45.877 --> 01:16:49.050
"about this in class, or
I was never taught it."

01:16:49.050 --> 01:16:51.690
And so I was like, I'm
going to start maybe reading

01:16:51.690 --> 01:16:53.640
about the history of the Second Amendment

01:16:53.640 --> 01:16:56.660
and it's its formulation.

01:16:56.660 --> 01:16:59.180
So I read this book or I'm like

01:16:59.180 --> 01:17:00.777
three quarters of the way through it.

01:17:00.777 --> 01:17:04.090
"The Second Amendment, a
Biography," by Michael Waldman

01:17:04.090 --> 01:17:07.600
not a brand new book I
think it was written in 2014

01:17:07.600 --> 01:17:09.550
won some awards is highly regarded

01:17:09.550 --> 01:17:13.260
as a great comprehensive
sort of exploration

01:17:13.260 --> 01:17:16.450
of the history of the Second Amendment.

01:17:16.450 --> 01:17:21.350
Both, I started realizing
there was so little that I knew

01:17:21.350 --> 01:17:26.350
and understood about the
formation of the Constitution

01:17:27.270 --> 01:17:29.400
and the Bill of Rights.

01:17:29.400 --> 01:17:32.260
I couldn't, I'm just going
to be honest with you,

01:17:32.260 --> 01:17:35.860
I couldn't have told you
that the Bill of Rights

01:17:35.860 --> 01:17:38.850
was like the first list of
amendments that was added

01:17:38.850 --> 01:17:40.610
to the constitution after it was written.

01:17:40.610 --> 01:17:43.553
- I think I could have told
you that, but only that.

01:17:43.553 --> 01:17:46.900
- Like I knew, I couldn't
have told you that

01:17:46.900 --> 01:17:49.260
and I didn't know exactly why it happened

01:17:49.260 --> 01:17:51.087
and what it was a reaction to

01:17:51.087 --> 01:17:53.363
and what the concerns were at the time.

01:17:54.580 --> 01:17:56.830
I was pretty ignorant of this.

01:17:56.830 --> 01:17:58.500
So if you're also ignorant of it

01:17:58.500 --> 01:18:01.630
or you just happen to be
interested in this kind of thing

01:18:01.630 --> 01:18:05.550
I recommend, it's a short
read and it's an easy read.

01:18:05.550 --> 01:18:08.187
It's not like super academic or anything.

01:18:08.187 --> 01:18:11.110
"The Second Amendment, a
Biography," by Michael Waldman

01:18:11.110 --> 01:18:12.980
just a nice introduction to this

01:18:12.980 --> 01:18:16.710
which I think then does
influence the way that we think

01:18:16.710 --> 01:18:21.710
about this very divisive and inflamed

01:18:24.240 --> 01:18:27.550
but very important debate that
we're having in our country

01:18:27.550 --> 01:18:30.980
as we're trying to navigate
being the country that leads

01:18:30.980 --> 01:18:34.910
in a lot of things, including
by a long shot and by

01:18:34.910 --> 01:18:38.064
a large margin, people who
get killed by gun violence.

01:18:38.064 --> 01:18:38.897
(relaxing music)

01:18:38.897 --> 01:18:42.854
- "The Second Amendment, a
Biography," Michael Waldman.

